WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.280
Cosmic ray muons reveal electrical fields in thunderstorms far stronger than direct measurements ever captured. This is Planetarian Daily, episode 201. The Grape's 3 muon telescope records 4 billion particles daily, and detects electric fields inside thunder clouds exceeding 100 million volts. A study published in the Journal of Cosmology and Astroparticle Physics explains an East-West detection asymmetry in the data.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:37.820
The asymmetry arises because the geomagnetic field steers positive and negative muons differently as primary cosmic rays pass through the magnetosphere.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:49.160
Grape's three sits in Uti India, and its 560-square meter detector captures the daily muon flux from a location where positive muons already outnumber negative ones.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:54.820
The telescope was never built for weather research, yet its sensitivity to electric fields

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.160
turns it into a remote sensor for atmospheric electricity.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.260
The Grape's three Mewon telescope records 4 billion Mewons daily, yet was never built

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.020
to study weather.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.300
Its sensitivity to thunderstorm electric field stems from how Earth's magnetic field

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.840
steers charged particles differently depending on arrival direction.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:20.000
The positive muons outnumber negative ones, creating a natural charge ratio probe that

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.680
amplifies small field effects.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:28.100
The new analysis shows the East-West detection asymmetry is an artifact of geomagnetic filtering

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.060
rather than any real difference in storm frequency.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:36.740
This turns an existing cosmic ray detector into a distributed sensor network for atmospheric

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.720
electricity without new hardware.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:44.420
Future work could combine multiple Mewon telescopes to map field strength across regions.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.700
Most people assume thunderstorms are mainly about rain, wind, and the occasional lightning

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:50.960
bolt we see from the ground.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.520
In reality, right now inside any active storm cell, charge separation between ice particles

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.200
and water droplets is building electric fields that can reach hundreds of millions of volts.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.260
The Grape's three telescope in India detects four billion Mewons every day.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:12.960
Those muons bend measurably when they pass through these fields, giving physicists a remote

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.240
readout of voltage differences far larger than anything measured by weather balloons.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:23.000
One memorable number is that the potential differences inside Thunderclouds exceed 100

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.840
million volts, roughly 10 times higher than the strongest fields recorded by aircraft instruments.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:35.500
This changes how we think about lightning initiation and also about radiation exposure for high altitude

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:35.920
flights.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:44.060
Next time you hear thunder, remember the invisible voltage structure above you is being mapped by particles that arrived from outer space.

